"AI progress and recommendations | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/ai-progress-and-recommendations)\nciteturn2314view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/ai-progress-and-recommendations\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/ai-progress-and-recommendations/; Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 6, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # AI progress and recommendations\nL17: \nL18: AI is unlocking new knowledge and capabilities. Our responsibility is to guide that power toward broad, lasting benefit.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Shared standards and insights from the frontier labs.\nL25:   * cite4†Shared standards and insights from the frontier labs.  L26:   * cite5†An approach to public oversight and accountability commensurate with capabilities, and that promotes positive impacts from AI and mitigates the negative ones.  L27:   * cite6†Building an AI resilience ecosystem.  L28:   * cite7†Ongoing reporting and measurement from the frontier labs and governments on the impacts of AI.  L29:   * cite8†Building for individual empowerment.  L30:   * cite4†Shared standards and insights from the frontier labs.  L31:   * cite5†An approach to public oversight and accountability commensurate with capabilities, and that promotes positive impacts from AI and mitigates the negative ones.  L32:   * cite6†Building an AI resilience ecosystem.  L33:   * cite7†Ongoing reporting and measurement from the frontier labs and governments on the impacts of AI.  L34:   * cite8†Building for individual empowerment.  L35: When the popular conception of the Turing test went whooshing by, many of us thought it was a little strange how much daily life just kept going. This was a milestone people had talked about for decades. It felt impossibly out of reach, then all of a sudden it felt close, then all of a sudden we were on the other side. We got some great new products and not much about the world changed, even though computers can now converse and think about hard problems.\nL36: Most of the world still thinks about AI as chatbots and better search, but today, we have systems that can outperform the smartest humans at some of our most challenging intellectual competitions. Although AI systems are still spikey and face serious weaknesses, systems that can solve such hard problems seem more like 80% of the way to an AI researcher than 20% of the way. The gap between how most people are using AI and what AI is presently capable of is immense.\nL37: AI systems that can discover new knowledge—either autonomously, or by making people more effective—are likely to have a significant impact on the world.\nL38: In just a few years, AI has gone from only being able to do tasks (in the realm of software engineering specifically) that a person can do in a few seconds to tasks that take a person more than an hour. We expect to have systems that can do tasks that take a person days or weeks soon; we do not know how to think about systems that can do tasks that would take a person centuries.\nL39: At the same time, the cost per unit of a given level of intelligence has fallen steeply; 40x per year is a reasonable estimate over the last few years!\nL40: \nL41: In 2026, we expect AI to be capable of making very small discoveries. In 2028 and beyond, we are pretty confident we will have systems that can make more significant discoveries (though we could of course be wrong, this is what our research progress appears to indicate).\nL42: We’ve long felt that AI progress plays out in surprising ways, and that society finds ways to co-evolve with the technology. Although we expect rapid and significant progress in AI capabilities in the next few years, we expect that day-to-day life will still feel surprisingly constant; the way we live has a lot of inertia even with much better tools.\nL43: In particular, we expect the future to provide new and hopefully better ways to live a fulfilling life, and for more people to experience such a life than do today. It is true that work will be different, the economic transition may be very difficult in some ways, and it is even possible that the fundamental socioeconomic contract will have to change. But in a world of widely-distributed abundance, people’s lives can be much better than they are today.\nL44: AI systems will help people understand their health, accelerate progress in fields like materials science, drug development, and climate modeling, and expand access to personalized education for students around the world. Demonstrating these kinds of tangible benefits helps build a shared vision of a world where AI can make life better, not just more efficient.\nL45: OpenAI is deeply cite9†committed to safety⁠ , which we think of as the practice of enabling AI’s positive impacts by mitigating the negative ones.\nL46: Although the potential upsides are enormous, we treat the risks of superintelligent systems as potentially catastrophic and believe that cite10†empirically⁠ cite11†studying⁠ cite12†safety⁠ and cite13†alignment⁠ can help global decisions, like whether the whole field should slow development to more carefully study these systems as we get closer to systems capable of recursive self-improvement.\nL47: Obviously, no one should deploy superintelligent systems without being able to robustly align and control them, and this requires more technical work.\nL48: Here are several things we think could help with achieving a positive future with AI:\nL49: ## Shared standards and insights from the frontier labs.\nL50: \nL51: We think that frontier labs should agree on shared safety principles and to cite14†share safety research⁠ , learnings about new risks, mechanisms to reduce race dynamics, and more. We can imagine ideas like frontier labs agreeing to certain standards around AI control evaluations being quite helpful.\nL52: \nL53: Society went through a similar process to establish building codes and fire standards, which have saved countless lives.\nL54: ## An approach to public oversight and accountability commensurate with capabilities, and that promotes positive impacts from AI and mitigates the negative ones.\nL55: There are two schools of thought about AI. One is that AI is like “normal technology,” in that it will progress like other technological revolutions in the past, from the printing press to the internet. Things will play out in ways that give people and society a chance to adapt, and conventional tools of public policy should work.\nL56: We will need to prioritize ideas like promoting innovation, protecting the privacy of conversations with AI and defending against misuse of powerful systems by bad actors by partnering with the federal government.\nL57: We believe AI at around today’s capability levels is roughly here, and should diffuse everywhere, which means most developers and open-source models, and almost all deployments of today’s technology, should have minimal additional regulatory burdens relative to what already exists. It certainly should not have to face a 50-state patchwork.\nL58: The other one is where superintelligence develops and diffuses in ways and at a speed humanity has not seen before. Here, we should do most of the things above, but we also will need to be more innovative. If the premise is that something like this will be difficult for society to adapt to in the “normal way,” we should also not expect typical regulation to be able to do much either.\nL59: In this case, we will probably need to work closely with the executive branch and related agencies of multiple countries (such as the various safety institutes) to coordinate well, particularly around areas such as mitigating AI applications to bioterrorism (and using AI to detect and prevent bioterrorism) and the implications of self-improving AI.\nL60: The high-order bit should be accountability to public institutions, but how we get there might have to differ from the past.\nL61: ## Building an AI resilience ecosystem.\nL62: In either scenario, building out an AI resilience ecosystem will be essential. When the internet emerged, we didn’t protect it with a single policy or company—we built an entire field of cybersecurity: software, encryption protocols, standards, monitoring systems, emergency response teams etc. That ecosystem didn’t eliminate risk, but it reduced it to a level society could live with, enabling people to trust digital infrastructure enough to build their lives and economies on it.\nL63: We will need something analogous for AI, and there is a powerful role for national governments to play in promoting industrial policy to encourage this.\nL64: ## Ongoing reporting and measurement from the frontier labs and governments on the impacts of AI.\nL65: \nL66: Understanding how AI is concretely impacting the world makes it easier to steer this technology towards positive impact. Prediction is hard: for example, the impact of AI on jobs has been hard to anticipate, in part because today’s AIs strengths and weaknesses are very different from those of humans. Measuring what’s happening in practice is likely to be very informative.\nL67: ## Building for individual empowerment.\nL68: \nL69: We believe that adults should be able to use AI on their own terms, within broad bounds defined by society. We expect access to advanced AI to be a foundational utility in the coming years—on par with electricity, clean water, or food. Ultimately, we think society should support making these tools widely available and that the north star should be helping empower people to achieve their goals.\nL70: \nL71:   * cite15†2025 L72: \nL73: ## Author\nL74: \nL75: OpenAI\nL76: ## Keep reading\nL77: \nL78: cite16†View all L79: \nL80: cite17†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L81: \nL82: cite18†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L83: \nL84: cite19†Introducing dots ProductSep 29, 2026 L85: \nL86: Research\nL87: \nL88:   * cite20†Research Index L89:   * cite21†Research Overview L90:   * cite22†Economic Research L91: \nL92: Latest Advancements\nL93: \nL94:   * cite23†GPT-6.1 Sol L95:   * cite24†GPT-6 Astra L96:   * cite25†GPT-5.6 L97:   * cite26†GPT-5.5 L98: \nL99: Safety\nL100:   * cite27†Safety Approach L101:   * cite28†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L102:   * cite29†Security & Privacy L103:   * cite30†Trust & Transparency L104: \nL105: Products\nL106:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L107:   * cite31†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L108:   * cite32†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L109:   * cite33†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L110:   * cite34†Codex†chatgpt.com L111:   * cite35†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L112:   * cite36†Release Notes L113: \nL114: API Platform\nL115: \nL116:   * cite37†Overview L117:   * cite38†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L118:   * cite39†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L119: \nL120: Business\nL121:   * cite40†Overview L122:   * cite41†Solutions L123:   * cite42†Resources L124:   * cite43†Plugins L125:   * cite44†Customer Stories L126:   * cite45†Partner Network L127:   * cite46†Contact Sales L128: \nL129: Developers\nL130: \nL131:   * cite47†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L132:   * cite48†Open Models L133:   * cite49†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L134:   * cite50†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L135:   * cite51†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L136: \nL137: Company\nL138:   * cite52†About Us L139:   * cite53†Our Charter L140:   * cite54†Careers L141:   * cite16†News L142: \nL143: Support\nL144: \nL145:   * cite55†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L146: \nL147: More\nL148: \nL149:   * cite56†Stories L150:   * cite57†Academy L151:   * cite58†Supply Co. L152:   * cite59†Livestreams L153:   * cite60†Podcast L154:   * cite61†RSS L155: \nL156: Terms & Policies\nL157: \nL158:   * cite62†Terms of Use L159:   * cite63†Privacy Policy L160:   * cite64†Other Policies L161: cite65†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite68†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite69†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite70†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite71†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L162: \nL163: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL164: \nL165: English United States\nL166: \nL167: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing the Teen Safety Blueprint | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-teen-safety-blueprint)\nciteturn2314view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-teen-safety-blueprint\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/introducing-the-teen-safety-blueprint/; Total lines: 127\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 6, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company cite4†Safety L16: # Introducing the Teen Safety Blueprint\nL17: \nL18: A framework for building AI that protects, empowers, and creates safer experiences for teens.\nL19: \nL20: cite5†Read the blueprint(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: We are introducing the cite5†Teen Safety Blueprint⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com , a roadmap for building AI tools responsibly and a practical starting point for policymakers who are working to set standards for teen use of AI. Young people deserve technology that expands opportunity and protects their well-being. The Blueprint helps define how AI should work for teens, including age-appropriate design, meaningful product safeguards, and ongoing research and evaluation.\nL26: The decisions made today will shape how teens use and are protected by this technology for years to come.\nL27: We aren’t waiting for regulation to catch up, we're putting this framework into action across our products. We’re anticipating risks and proactively strengthening protections for young people. In recent months we have strengthened safeguards for younger users, launched cite6†parental controls⁠ with proactive notifications, and we’re building toward an cite7†age-prediction system⁠ to understand if someone is under 18 so that their ChatGPT experience can be tailored appropriately.\nL28: This is ongoing work, and there is more to do. We are committed to building AI responsibly and improving continuously as we learn alongside parents, experts, and teens. We welcome collaboration from others working toward the same goal.\nL29: \nL30:   * cite8†2025 L31:   * cite9†User Safety & Control L32: ## Author\nL33: \nL34: OpenAI\nL35: ## Keep reading\nL36: \nL37: cite10†View all L38: \nL39: cite11†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L40: \nL41: cite12†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L42: \nL43: cite13†Introducing dots ProductSep 29, 2026 L44: \nL45: Research\nL46: \nL47:   * cite14†Research Index L48:   * cite15†Research Overview L49:   * cite16†Economic Research L50: \nL51: Latest Advancements\nL52: \nL53:   * cite17†GPT-6.1 Sol L54:   * cite18†GPT-6 Astra L55:   * cite19†GPT-5.6 L56:   * cite20†GPT-5.5 L57: \nL58: Safety\nL59:   * cite21†Safety Approach L60:   * cite22†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L61:   * cite23†Security & Privacy L62:   * cite24†Trust & Transparency L63: \nL64: Products\nL65:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L66:   * cite25†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L67:   * cite26†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L68:   * cite27†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L69:   * cite28†Codex†chatgpt.com L70:   * cite29†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L71:   * cite30†Release Notes L72: \nL73: API Platform\nL74: \nL75:   * cite31†Overview L76:   * cite32†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L77:   * cite33†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L78: \nL79: Business\nL80:   * cite34†Overview L81:   * cite35†Solutions L82:   * cite36†Resources L83:   * cite37†Plugins L84:   * cite38†Customer Stories L85:   * cite39†Partner Network L86:   * cite40†Contact Sales L87: \nL88: Developers\nL89: \nL90:   * cite41†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L91:   * cite42†Open Models L92:   * cite43†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L93:   * cite44†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L94:   * cite45†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L95: \nL96: Company\nL97:   * cite46†About Us L98:   * cite47†Our Charter L99:   * cite48†Careers L100:   * cite10†News L101: \nL102: Support\nL103: \nL104:   * cite49†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L105: \nL106: More\nL107: \nL108:   * cite50†Stories L109:   * cite51†Academy L110:   * cite52†Supply Co. L111:   * cite53†Livestreams L112:   * cite54†Podcast L113:   * cite55†RSS L114: \nL115: Terms & Policies\nL116: \nL117:   * cite56†Terms of Use L118:   * cite57†Privacy Policy L119:   * cite58†Other Policies L120: cite59†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L121: \nL122: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL123: \nL124: English United States\nL125: \nL126: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1 million business customers: the fastest-growing business platform in history | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/1-million-businesses-putting-ai-to-work)\nciteturn2314view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/1-million-businesses-putting-ai-to-work\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/1-million-businesses-putting-ai-to-work/; Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 5, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # 1 million business customers: the fastest-growing business platform in history\nL17: \nL18: Customers such as Amgen, Commonwealth Bank, Booking.com, Cisco, Lowe’s, Morgan Stanley, T-Mobile, Target, and Thermo Fisher Scientific are already on board—with more joining every week.\nL19: \nL20: cite4†Contact sales L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: \nL26: From personal use to companywide impact\nL27:   * cite5†From personal use to companywide impact L28:   * cite6†New tools, integrations, and capabilities for businesses L29:   * cite7†Companies are seeing meaningful ROI   L30:   * cite8†Where we’re heading next L31: \nL32:   * cite5†From personal use to companywide impact L33:   * cite6†New tools, integrations, and capabilities for businesses L34:   * cite7†Companies are seeing meaningful ROI   L35:   * cite8†Where we’re heading next L36: Today, we’re announcing that more than 1 million business customers around the world are directly using OpenAI—the fastest-growing business platform in history. This includes all organizations that actively pay OpenAI for business use—either through ChatGPT for Work, or through direct consumption of our models through our developer platform.\nL37: We’re proud to work with category leaders in industries like financial services, healthcare, retail, and more, where our technology is making intelligence central to their customer experiences, internal operations, and team-level workflows.\nL38: ## From personal use to companywide impact\nL39: Our enterprise momentum is fueled in part by consumer adoption. With more than 800 million weekly users already familiar with ChatGPT, adoption and ROI within businesses is realized more rapidly—pilots are shorter and rollouts face less friction. We’re seeing this with the growth of ChatGPT for Work—we now have more than 7 million total ChatGPT for Work seats (up 40% in just cite9†2 months⁠(opens in a new window)†x.com ). ChatGPT Enterprise seats specifically have grown 9x year-over-year.\nL40: ## New tools, integrations, and capabilities for businesses\nL41: \nL42: To support this acceleration, we’ve launched a new wave of tools, integrations, and capabilities to help teams move from early use to full-scale deployment:\nL43: \nL44:   * We introduced cite10†company knowledge , which lets ChatGPT reason across tools like Slack, SharePoint, Google Drive, GitHub, and more to get answers, do analysis, and take action using a version of GPT‑5 that’s optimized for working with tools and providing citations.\nL45:   * Adoption for cite11†Codex , our model for code generation, refactoring, and workflow automation has accelerated quickly—usage is up 10x since August. Recently, organizations like Cisco have rolled out Codex into their engineering workflows, cutting code review times by 50% and shrinking project timelines from weeks to days.\nL46:   * We also made enterprise agents practical to build and deploy with cite12†AgentKit , making it possible for teams to go from idea to production in days instead of months. Companies like Carlyle are already seeing results: the AgentKit evaluation platform cut development time on Carlyle’s multi-agent due diligence framework by over 50% and improved agent accuracy by 30%.\nL47:   * We also advanced our multimodal models to enable richer workflows—from the cite13†Image Generation API and cite14†Sora 2 for visual and video creation to cite15†gpt-realtime and Realtime API to build production voice agents. Teams across every industry can now work across text, images, video, and audio in one system.\nL48: ## Companies are seeing meaningful ROI\nL49: \nL50: AI is cite16†delivering real business value⁠(opens in a new window)†x.com . According to a cite17†recent Wharton study⁠(opens in a new window)†ai.wharton.upenn.edu , 75% of enterprises report a positive ROI, and fewer than 5% report a negative return. While there are many studies on this topic, this one reflects what we see on the ground today with our customers: when AI is deployed with the right use case and infrastructure, teams see real results.\nL51:   * cite18†Indeed⁠ is using OpenAI APIs in its Invite to Apply feature to drive a 20% increase in applications and a 13% uplift in downstream success.\nL52: \nL53:   * cite19†Lowe’s empowers all associates in 1,700+ stores with expert project guidance through Mylow Companion, an in-store app built with OpenAI models.\nL54: \nL55:   * With OpenAI as the backbone of Fin, their customer service agent, cite20†Intercom has accelerated development cycles from quarters to days.\nL56:   * And starting today, cite21†Databricks⁠(opens in a new window)†databricks.com is bringing OpenAI frontier intelligence to where enterprises’ data already lives—making it easier to build and run high-quality agents.\nL57: ## Where we’re heading next\nL58: \nL59: Beyond internal adoption, we’re seeing a second trend: businesses increasingly want to build with OpenAI too.\nL60: Companies are creating new applications and agentic workflows directly on our platform. Canva, Figma, Zillow, Spotify, and others have cite22†plugged their apps directly into ChatGPT to meet users where they already are. Shopify, Etsy, Walmart, PayPal, and Salesforce are building new shopping experiences through the cite23†Agentic Commerce Protocol (ACP) in ChatGPT, bringing conversational commerce into the flow of everyday decisions.\nL61: We’re deeply grateful to the customers building with us today—and we’re looking forward to what’s next in 2026 and beyond. There’s a big opportunity to rethink the operating system for work, and we’re excited to build the platform to power it.\nL62: \nL63:   * cite24†2025 L64: ## Author\nL65: \nL66: OpenAI\nL67: ## Keep reading\nL68: \nL69: cite25†View all L70: \nL71: cite26†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L72: \nL73: cite27†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L74: \nL75: cite28†Introducing dots ProductSep 29, 2026 L76: \nL77: Re